## Item Headings

Standard headings receive auto-generated slugs for deep links, wikilinks, and the table of contents. Preceding a heading with a bullet marker (`*`, `-`, `•`) turns it into an **item heading** — a glossary-style term:

### * Collision detection

Median-heuristic crossing reduction + bounding-box overlap validation keep diagram
edges from overlapping nodes (`checkBoxesOverlap`).

### * Search & replace

Document search maps every `<mark>` hit back onto matching SVG elements using
`data-label-ord`, adding `.is-hit` / `.is-current` classes to nodes and cells.

### * Item headings are resolvable

Item headings participate in the same 4-pass resolver, so [[Collision detection]] and [[Search & replace]] are real links.

---

## Wikilink Resolution

Wikilinks resolve through an extension fast-path and a 4-pass fuzzy heading match, then fall back to the assets directory:

- **Direct** — [[Item Headings]] links to its exact heading.
- **Aliased** — [[Callout Alerts|the callout section]] links with custom display text.
- **Fuzzy (typo-tolerant)** — [[Wikilink Reslution]] still resolves here thanks to the Levenshtein pass (`wikilink reslution` → `wikilink resolution`).
- **Asset image** — [[mdpp-logo.svg]] embeds the demo logo as a base64 data URI in `--single` mode:
- **Literal in code** — `` `[[not-a-link]]` `` inside backticks is never treated as a link.

---

## File Links & Missing Targets

Markdown links keep standard GFM behaviour, with two extensions:

- Unsafe `javascript:` / `vbscript:` schemes are rejected; http(s), mailto, ftp?, and relative URLs pass through.
- `file:///` links are verified against the filesystem and **warn instead of failing**: [make-believe spec](file:///SHOWCASE_NONEXISTENT_QUIET.txt) points at a file that does not exist, so compiling this page emits the non-fatal warning `File link target not found: "file:///SHOWCASE_NONEXISTENT_QUIET.txt"` yet still renders the anchor.

```powershell
mdd showcase/showcase.mdd --single -o showcase/showcase.html
# File link target not found: "file:///SHOWCASE_NONEXISTENT_QUIET.txt"
```

Fuzzy path repair is also built in: `00_DEV` / `00__DEV` segment mismatches in a link are corrected automatically when the corrected path exists on disk.

---

## Escapes & Literals

- **Escaped wikilink** — `\[[literal]]` renders as `[[literal]]` with no resolution attempt.
- **Literal em-dash** — in labels, `\—` renders a real `—` without splitting title/subtitle (see the diagram in the next section).
- **HTML not allowed** — raw HTML is disabled (`html: false`); a literal `<sup>raw html</sup>` shows up as escaped text on this page.

Backslashes before markdown punctuation behave per GFM.

---

## Callout Alerts

> [!]
> A bare highlight callout — no title banner, `[!]`.

> [!NOTE]
> Information callout. Blockquotes starting with `[!]` or `[!IDENTIFIER]` become `.alert` boxes.

> [!WARNING]
> Any identifier is accepted and kept — case-preserved — as the title banner. `[!WARNING]` is just one example.

> [!KEY TAKEAWAY]
> Multi-line callouts keep nested markdown:
> - lists
> - **bold**, `code`, and [[Wikilink Resolution|wikilinks]] inside callouts
> - and so on.

---

## Diagram DSL

The `diagram` fence compiles to a responsive, searchable SVG. This section exercises every syntax form.

### Node Shapes & Edge Forms

```diagram TB
TITLE: Node Shapes & Edge Forms
%% comments are stripped and ignored
BARE
ROUND(Rounded — processes)
DECIDE{Decision — diamond}
DECIDE -- labelled prose --> RECT[Rectangle — sharp corners]
RECT -->|directed + label| ROUND
ROUND --- BARE
```

- **Shapes**: `[rect]`, `(rounded)`, `{diamond}`, and a bare token that defaults to a rectangle.
- **Edge forms**: `-->`, `---`, `-->|label|`, and `A -- label --> B`.
- **Title–subtitle split**: ` — ` splits bold title from muted subtitle on every node above.
- **Fence argument**: ` ```diagram TB ` sets the direction inline.

### Directions

All five directions are valid — as a fence argument, a bare in-body token, or a `DIRECTION:` directive:

```diagram LR
A[Left] --> B[Right] 
```

```diagram RL
A[Right-to-left] --> B[Reversed ranks]
```

```diagram BT
A[Bottom] --> B[Top first]
```

```diagram
DIRECTION: auto
A[Abstract] --> B[Concrete]
```

`auto` renders **top-to-bottom on narrow viewports** and **left-to-right on wide ones** — resize the window to see it flip.

### Text Wrapping

Long labels are word-wrapped at constant char width to fit the widest line:

```diagram
TITLE: Text Wrapping
ROLE[“Pipeline Orchestrator — responsible for coordinating ingestion, transformation, enrichment, and final distribution across downstream tenants”]
```

### Cyclic Flow

```diagram
TITLE: Cyclic Flow — Return Arcs
QUEUE[QUEUE — pending work items]
WORKER[WORKER — consumes and retries]
QUEUE --> WORKER
WORKER -->|requeues failures| QUEUE
WORKER --> DONE[COMPLETED]
```

The back-edge `WORKER → QUEUE` is detected by DFS colouring, excluded from rank assignment, and **drawn as a return arc** below the graph. Compiling warns `Diagram contains 1 cyclic edge(s); normalized to a DAG for layout (drawn as return arcs).`

### Self-Loop

```diagram
TITLE: Self Loop
PROCESSOR[Processor] --> PROCESSOR
```

Self-loops are handled the same way — excluded from ranking, drawn as a side arc that clears the node width.

### Crossing Reduction

```diagram
TITLE: Crossing Reduction
ROOT[Root] --> LEFT[Left branch]
ROOT --> MID[Mid branch]
ROOT --> RIGHT[Right branch]
LEFT --> OUT[Outcome]
MID --> OUT
RIGHT --> OUT
```

Three in, one out — longest-path ranking plus median order within each rank keeps the SVG legible.

---

## Table DSL

GTFM-style pipe tables in a `table` fence render as interactive SVGs with a `TITLE:` header, custom scrollbars, and search-match highlighting:

```table
TITLE: Table DSL — SVG Pipe Tables
| Feature | Syntax | Rendering |
|---------|--------|-----------|
| Default column | plain | left-aligned |
| Left marker | :--- | alignment markers ignored → left-aligned |
| Centre marker | :---: | alignment markers ignored → left-aligned |
| Right marker | ---: | alignment markers ignored → left-aligned |
| Empty cell | (blank cell below) | blank surface |
|  |  |  |
| Long cell | Markdown++ compiles pipe tables into interactive SVG output with search synchronised highlighting across nodes and cells | wraps within the cell |
```

---

## Standard Markdown (GFM)

Everything GFM provides still works inside a `.mdd` document:

- **Unordered lists**, *ordered lists*, `inline code`, **bold**, *italic*, and ~~strikethrough~~.
- **Task lists**:
  - [x] compiled to HTML
  - [ ] open task
- **Blockquotes**, horizontal rules, and nested content:

> A blockquote with a nested list:
> 1. first
> 2. second

---

**Raw HTML is escaped**, e.g. `<sup>raw html</sup>` → `&lt;sup&gt;`.

---

## Mirror Mode & Dual-Mode Artifact

Markdown++ produces a dual-mode artifact with two complementary reading layers:

1. **Read Mode**: Clean, publication-grade reading experience with in-margin peek pills (`🪞 Q&A` / `🪞 Semantic Probe`) that keep the main narrative flow clean and undisturbed.
2. **Mirror Mode**: An explicit reader-side semantic audit layer where the reader reconstructs the author's declared conceptual map, answers targeted probes about it, and exposes divergences between the reader's reconstruction and the author's declared meaning.

Toggle modes using the topbar switch or with <kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>M</kbd>.

```mirror qa
TITLE: Markdown++ Dual-Mode Architecture
Q: What is the difference between Read Mode and Mirror Mode?
A: Read Mode preserves an undisturbed, publication-grade reading experience with in-margin peek pills. Mirror Mode fully expands all semantic probes, Q&A blocks, and reader audit tools across the document.

Q: How does client-side state persist?
A: Probe selections, alignment statuses, and reader questions persist locally in browser localStorage. You can reset all saved state at any time via the Appearance settings modal.
```

```mirror probe
TITLE: Semantic Audit vs Compliance Test
PROBE: What constitutes a successful reader engagement in Mirror Mode?
[ ] Achieving 100% agreement on all author claims.
[x] Accurate reconstruction of the author's declared distinction, even if the reader contests the premise --> Mirror Mode separates understanding from agreement.
[ ] Automatically compiling prose assertions into machine tests.

AUTHOR: The author distinguishes conceptual comprehension from ideological agreement. A reader who accurately maps the author's declared distinction has successfully mirrored the document, regardless of whether they agree with the author's premise.
DIVERGENCE: Confusing semantic understanding with compliance turns an intellectual audit into an ideological test.
```

```mirror clarification
TITLE: Semantic Audit Clarification
Q: Is a disagreement with the author considered a failure in Mirror Mode?
A: No. A reader who demonstrates they understand the author's declared distinction, but chooses to contest the premise, is fully aligned in Mirror Mode ("I understand you. I disagree with you" is a successful mirror).
```

---

## Runtime: Search, Theme & Navigation

Everything above is not just static markup — the compiled page ships a runtime:

- **Dual-mode toggle** — switch between publication Read Mode and semantic Mirror Mode using the topbar switch or <kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>M</kbd>; in Read Mode, margin pills peek open without moving your reading position; responses and reader notes persist privately in localStorage with instant reset in Appearance settings.
- **Search inside an SVG** — type `Prose` or `Collision` into the search box above: the matching diagram node/cell lights up with `.is-hit`, and `Prev` / `Next` cycle through results (`N / M` counter). Case-sensitivity toggle available.
- **Live theme** — toggle dark/light with the moon button; every fill inherits `var(--accent)` and friends.
- **Live accent** — open the appearance panel and drag the rainbow picker (or tap a swatch): node borders, edges, headers, favicon, and brand logo recolour instantly.
- **Navigation history** — click a wikilink (e.g. to [[Item Headings]]) then press <kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>←</kbd> / <kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>→</kbd>: the pill restores scroll position and search state; <kbd>Esc</kbd> steps back.
- **Deep links** — each heading and alert carries an anchor (`#wikilink-resolution`); the copy-link button on hover writes the URL.
- **Back to top** — the floating button + scroll progress bar in the header track your place.
- **Mobile** — ≤900px collapses the sidebar to a drawer; diagrams/tables overflow with 2px smooth scrollbars.

